-- title:
--   Global labels of retained tree nodes
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Consider the tree extracted from the candidate stages.
--
--   1. **Retained candidate.** Each child of a level is identified, through the positive-support embeddings concatenated in parent order, with its position in the complete candidate sequence of that level. It keeps its parent.
--   2. **Candidate label.** A nonroot node $v$ at level $k+1$ gets
--   $$
--   \mathrm{label}(v)=\mathrm{base}_k+\mathrm{position}(v),
--   $$
--   its original global ordinal. Labels of a level lie below the next base, earlier levels precede later bases, and the labelling is injective.
